WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.330
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.920
In this series, we talk about research on
all kinds of physics simulations, including

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.460
fluids, collision physics, and we have even
ventured into hair simulations.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:23.000
We mostly talk about how the individual hair
strands should move, and how they should look,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.500
in terms of color and reflectance.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.090
Creating these beautiful videos takes getting
many-many moving parts right, for instance,

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:35.629
before all of that, the very first step is
not any of those steps.

00:00:35.629 --> 00:00:41.100
First, we have to get the 3D geometry of these
hairstyles into our simulation system.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.710
In a previous episode, we have seen an excellent
work that does this well for human hair.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:52.859
But what if we would like to model not human
hair, but something completely different?

00:00:52.859 --> 00:00:58.510
Well, hold on to your papers, because this
new work is so general, that it can look at

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:06.040
an input image or video, and give us not only
a model of the human hair, but human skin,

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.040
garments, and of course, my favorite, smoke
plumes, and more.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.120
But if you look here, this part begs the following
question - the input is an image, and the

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:21.750
output also looks like an image, and we need
to make them similar - so what’s the big

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.750
deal here?

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.840
A copying machine can do that, no?

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.490
Well, not really.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:28.490
Here’s why.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:34.100
On the output, we are working with something
that indeed looks like an image, but it is

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.280
not an image.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:42.040
It is a 3 dimensional cube, in which we have
to specify color and opacity values everywhere.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.480
After that, we simulate rays of light passing
through this volume, which is a technique

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.150
that we call ray marching, and this process
has to produce the same 2D image through ray

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.050
marching as what was given as an input.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.390
That’s much, much harder than building a
copying machine.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.880
As you see here, normally, this does not work
well at all, because, for instance, a standard

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.140
algorithm sees lights in the background, and
it assumes that these are really bright and

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.540
dense points.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.849
That is kind of true, but they are usually
not even part of the data we would like to

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:17.849
reconstruct.

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:22.640
To solve this issue, the authors propose learning
to tell the foreground and background images

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.549
apart, so they can be separated before we
start the reconstruction of the human.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:33.739
And this is a good research paper, which means
that if it contains multiple new techniques,

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:39.930
each of them are tested separately to know
how much they contribute to the final results.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:45.221
We get the previously seen, dreadful results
without the background separation step, here

00:02:45.221 --> 00:02:49.859
are the results with the learned backgrounds,
we can still see the lights due to the way

00:02:49.859 --> 00:02:55.109
the final image is constructed, and the fact
that we have so little of this halo effect

00:02:55.109 --> 00:02:56.749
is really cool.

00:02:56.749 --> 00:03:01.430
Here you see the results with the true background
data where the background learning step is

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.799
not present.

00:03:02.799 --> 00:03:08.540
Note that this is cheating, because this data
is not available for all cameras and backgrounds,

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:13.420
however, it is a great way to test the quality
of this learning step.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.349
The comparison of the learned method against
this reveals that the two are very close,

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:21.709
which is exactly what we are looking for.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:24.959
And finally, the input footage is also shown
for reference.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:30.060
This is ultimately what we are trying to achieve,
and as you see, the output is quite close

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.060
to it.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.930
As you see here, the final algorithm excels
at reconstructing volume data for toys, smoke

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.280
plumes, and humans alike.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.739
And the coolest part is that it works for
not only stationary inputs, but for animations

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:44.739
as well.

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:50.400
Wait, actually, there is something that is
perhaps even cooler, with the magic of neural

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.810
networks and latent spaces, we can even animate
this data.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:00.450
Here you see an example of that where an avatar
is animated in real-time by moving around

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:01.769
this magenta dot.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:07.489
A limiting factor here is the resolution of
this reconstruction - if you look closely,

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:11.969
you see that some fine details are missing,
but you know the saying…given the rate of

00:04:11.969 --> 00:04:16.609
progress in machine learning research, two
more papers down the line, and this will likely

00:04:16.609 --> 00:04:19.120
be orders of magnitude better.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.139
If you feel that you always need to take your
daily dose of papers, my statistics show that

